WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.091
A tool definition is a contract.

2
00:00:02.371 --> 00:00:04.504
You describe each tool with three fields.

3
00:00:04.784 --> 00:00:07.173
A name, which is how the model refers to it.

4
00:00:07.453 --> 00:00:10.717
A description, which is how the model decides when to use it.

5
00:00:10.997 --> 00:00:16.075
And an input_schema, a JSON Schema object that says what arguments the tool takes.

6
00:00:16.355 --> 00:00:24.227
JSON Schema is a standard vocabulary for describing JSON: types, required fields, enums, and nested objects.

7
00:00:24.507 --> 00:00:27.259
Treat the description as a prompt, because it is one.

8
00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:41.064
It is loaded into the model’s context on every request, together with a short tool-use system prompt the API adds for you (a few hundred tokens on current Claude models, per the pricing section of the tool use docs).

9
00:00:41.344 --> 00:00:48.555
Anthropic’s guidance is to say what the tool does, when to call it, what each parameter means, and what the tool will not do.

10
00:00:48.835 --> 00:00:50.712
Lesson 11 goes deep on this.

11
00:00:50.992 --> 00:00:53.232
Two schema settings matter from day one.

12
00:00:53.512 --> 00:00:58.461
Mark the fields you need as required, and use enum when a value comes from a fixed list.

13
00:00:58.741 --> 00:01:06.187
And consider strict: true on the tool, which makes the API constrain generation so the arguments always match your schema.

14
00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:09.112
Strict mode guarantees the shape of the input.

15
00:01:09.392 --> 00:01:16.347
It does not guarantee the input is true: a perfectly formed email address can still belong to nobody on your team.
